[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Custom Views and Canvas Drawing in Android (Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"While Android provides a rich set of built-in UI components, there are times when standard widgets fall short of unique design requirements. In these scenarios, creating a Custom View and drawing directly onto a Canvas allows you to build highly optimized, custom-tailored graphics and interactive user interfaces."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Lifecycle of a Custom View"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before writing code, it is essential to understand the three core phases of drawing a View in Android: Measuring (onMeasure), Layout (onLayout), and Drawing (onDraw). When dealing with custom Canvas drawing, the onDraw(Canvas canvas) method is where you implement your rendering logic."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Creating the Custom View Class"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a new Java class that extends the base View class. You will need to override the constructors to ensure your view can be instantiated both programmatically and via XML layouts."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"public class CircleProgressBar extends View {
    private Paint paint;
    private int progress = 0;

    public CircleProgressBar(Context context) {
        super(context);
        init();
    }

    public CircleProgressBar(Context context, AttributeSet attrs) {
        super(context, attrs);
        init();
    }

    private void init() {
        paint = new Paint(Paint.ANTI_ALIAS_FLAG);
        paint.setColor(Color.BLUE);
        paint.setStyle(Paint.Style.STROKE);
        paint.setStrokeWidth(20f);
    }"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Implementing the onDraw Method"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Canvas object provides methods for drawing shapes, text, and bitmaps, while the Paint object defines how those elements are rendered (colors, thickness, anti-aliasing). Here is how you draw a simple arc representing progress."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"@Override
protected void onDraw(Canvas canvas) {
    super.onDraw(canvas);

    int width = getWidth();
    int height = getHeight();
    int radius = Math.min(width, height) / 2 - 30;

    RectF rectF = new RectF(
        width / 2f - radius,
        height / 2f - radius,
        width / 2f + radius,
        height / 2f + radius
    );

    // Draw background track
    paint.setColor(Color.LIGHT_GRAY);
    canvas.drawArc(rectF, 0, 360, false, paint);

    // Draw active progress
    paint.setColor(Color.RED);
    float sweepAngle = (progress / 100f) * 360f;
    canvas.drawArc(rectF, -90, sweepAngle, false, paint);
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Exposing Control Methods and Invalidating"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To update the View dynamically, create setter methods that modify internal states and trigger a redraw using invalidate(). Calling invalidate() informs the Android framework that the view needs to be redrawn, which triggers onDraw() again."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public void setProgress(int progress) {
    if (progress >= 0 && progress <= 100) {
        this.progress = progress;
        invalidate(); // Requests a call to onDraw
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Custom Views"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid object allocation inside onDraw(): Creating new Paint or Rect objects inside onDraw() causes excessive garbage collection, leading to UI stutter and dropped frames."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always enable anti-aliasing: Initialize your Paint objects with Paint.ANTI_ALIAS_FLAG to ensure smooth edges on shapes and text."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Handle padding properly: Make sure your drawing logic respects the view's padding by utilizing getPaddingLeft(), getPaddingTop(), etc."}]
